{
  "game": "Romancing SaGa 2: Revenge of the Seven",
  "section": "Armors",
  "source_language": "English",
  "data_notes": [
    "Item names and source descriptions are preserved in English.",
    "Missing values are omitted rather than inferred.",
    "Repeated entries and apparent source typos are retained as supplied.",
    "The source distinguishes Shields from Guards; see the category notes."
  ],
  "general_notes": [
    {
      "title": "Shields and Guards use the same equipment slot",
      "text": "A character can equip either a Shield or a Guard. They are different armor types but occupy the same equipment slot. They are usually distinguished by the affixes Shield and Guard, although there are exceptions such as Buckler, which is a Guard."
    },
    {
      "title": "Block rate",
      "text": "Shields and Guards have a Block Rate expressed as a percentage chance to block. Using Defend on a turn can increase it. Certain Formations have a tank position that automatically makes the character Defend after attacking, increasing the block rate and the chance to trigger the Shield or Guard."
    },
    {
      "title": "How Guards work",
      "text": "Guards nullify damage. The English localization mistranslates this as Block in the Phys. Defense section of the item data screen. All Guards nullify Physical Melee damage, and some can also nullify Ranged Physical damage and elemental damage, including spell damage (for example, Will Guard)."
    },
    {
      "title": "How Shields work",
      "text": "When a Shield block triggers, it temporarily increases the character's Physical Defense, reducing damage taken during that block. It does not permanently increase the Armor Physical Defense value while equipped. Some Shields, such as Earth Guard, temporarily increase certain elemental defenses when a block occurs."
    },
    {
      "title": "Remake behavior and source opinion",
      "text": "The source states that in earlier versions Shields were better against multi-hit attacks because a Guard only nullified the first hit. In the Remake, a Guard nullifies every hit in a multi-hit attack. The source therefore considers Shields to have no meaningful purpose in the Remake and says even a Buckler with a 20% block rate is superior to the best Shield, because it completely nullifies Physical Melee damage, even against the final boss."
    },
    {
      "title": "Two-handed weapons and blocking",
      "text": "A character using two-handed weapons, including Martial Arts, bows, Greatsword or Spear techniques, cannot block for the rest of the round after attacking with one of these weapons. Characters casting spells can block with a Shield as long as they are equipped with a one-handed weapon."
    }
  ],
